A||||10||ejayerik||The best way to clean an engine still inside the vehice||||5||
Z||000000||ejayerik||12-07-2001||08:26 PM||||Hey guys and galls. Any suggestions on how I should cover the ins & outs on my engine when I clean it? I don't want to spot the money for a steam clean and I have a relitivly good method of spray washing it. (so I believe) Should I leave the Fiero running while I clean it? Anything I should know about methods of covering items? I've got an 85 2M4 5-speed. Don't want to drown it..lol       <BR>                                Thanks, Erik||||reg||5
Z||000001||rogergarrison||12-08-2001||05:51 AM||||just use a car wash. keep the spray down to minimum on electronic parts like tps, map, distributor, def off the alternator (ive blown 2). I try to keep mine running, but no big deal if it quits. I do it only after the engine is totally warmed up, so it dries out pretty fast. Plastic bag or rag around distributor wouldnt hurt.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Eric_PA||12-08-2001||08:32 AM||||Try using Castrol Super clean, but make sure you dilute it a bit first before spraying it on the engine. Sray it on, let it set for a few minutes and spray it off. I was surprised when I used that stuff, it works great.<P>Eric||||reg||1
Z||000003||Eriamjh||12-08-2001||08:32 AM||||Other than covering up critical electronic parts, spraying it as described above works.<P>A tip I learned a few years ago was to spray the rubber and such with "No-Touch" tire cleaner.  It leaves a nice shine and doesn't require a wipe down!  <P>Works for tires, works for engines!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Banzai||12-08-2001||08:51 AM||||Try using Simple Green Automotive, in the black bottle.  It isn't as harsh as Castrol Super clean and it works just as good while at the same time leaving a better finish. Clean the engine cold. If you can hook a hose to your laundry tub and get hot water it will be the best.  Clean from top to bottom. Spray with water then spray down with cleaner.  Use a long bristled brush(4-5"bristles)(body brush, blue bristles or scrub brush with yellow bristles) and scrub everything you can get to. Rinse WELL. dry with compressed air. Then spray the whole engine compartment down with Wet and Black-(avoiding the belts)let soak for 30 minutes then wipe off the excess. You will be impressed.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mickey_Moose||12-08-2001||09:59 AM||||I use Gold Matrix degreaser...it's a biodegradable degreaser that is used on food processing equipment. Best one that I have seen...oil and dirt don't stand up to it at all (just spray on let sit a minute and rinse off).<P>ALWAYS wash an engine when it is cold...cold water on a warm block may create problems in the form of cracks.<P>Tim<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000006||rogergarrison||12-08-2001||12:08 PM||||Ive never heard of cracking it washing it. You use hot water and its only on the outside. Out of prob hundreds of engines I cleaned, ive never had that happen ever. What happens when you drive it in the rain, and it cold outside. Wed have lots of busted engines. Even the 'standard' engine cleaner say use on warm engine. Just my opinion, though.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tina||12-08-2001||01:42 PM||||A couple of cans of engine degreaser and<BR>a gardenhose seem to do the trick for me.<BR>(these new covers with elastic bands from Ziplock work well for stuff that you don't want to get wet)<BR>Spray everthing in sight,then do it all over for good measure  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Rinse, and dry with airhose and or paper towels.<P>Tina<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||DXR_DAD||12-08-2001||06:31 PM||||Do a search, Key word: "Clean engine compartments"<P>Lots of pics of some very clean & nice engines!<P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||1
Z||000009||88CoupeV6||12-08-2001||06:48 PM||||I would let it cool some as getting water on the hot manifold will cause it to crack or weaken at the welds. Trust me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000010||ejayerik||12-08-2001||07:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ejayerik:<BR><B>Hey guys and galls. Any suggestions on how I should cover the ins & outs on my engine when I clean it? I don't want to spot the money for a steam clean and I have a relitivly good method of spray washing it. (so I believe) Should I leave the Fiero running while I clean it? Anything I should know about methods of covering items? I've got an 85 2M4 5-speed. Don't want to drown it..lol       <BR>                                Thanks, Erik</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hot, cold, warm,? Hmmmm. I think I will drive it, let it cool until it is slightly warm, and avoid the manafold. Sound good guys? I can get warm water but not hot. And it seems I will be spending a fortune on soap. BTW, good idea to use the Relnolds wraps things. I'm sure they will come in handy. I'll let you know if I drowned it cause I'm goint to atemp it this weekend. It may rain, perhaps I should just leave the deck lid open and spray in some soap. lol<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR>||||reg||4
